\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces A known structural allowance increases honest length and protects against departures that invalidate exact-model inference. Four binary modes have caps $(1,2,1)$, $n=2048$, $\lambda =3$, $\sigma =0.2$, and $A=E_{1111}$. The left panel evaluates length at $B_0$ and the right coverage at $B_0+\eta A$, each with 2,000 replications; length bars are $\pm 1.96$ Monte Carlo standard errors and coverage bars are Wilson 95\% Monte Carlo intervals.}}{15}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:tube}{{3}{15}{A known structural allowance increases honest length and protects against departures that invalidate exact-model inference. Four binary modes have caps $(1,2,1)$, $n=2048$, $\lambda =3$, $\sigma =0.2$, and $A=E_{1111}$. The left panel evaluates length at $B_0$ and the right coverage at $B_0+\eta A$, each with 2,000 replications; length bars are $\pm 1.96$ Monte Carlo standard errors and coverage bars are Wilson 95\% Monte Carlo intervals}{figure.3}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {7.7}Audit addendum: independent finite-variance noise}{15}{subsection.7.7}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces The energy certificate reduces length while the multiscale normalization tracks loading transitions. The coefficient is $\epsilon =\kappa t_n^2/\lambda ^2$ in the prespecified loading $A_\epsilon $. Each sample-size cohort contains 2,000 datasets reused across loadings and methods. Bars are pointwise $\pm 1.96$ Monte Carlo standard errors; ratios use paired influence-variable errors. The horizontal line in the right panel is equal mean length. The left panel is normalized by a rate scale, not an exact optimal confidence constant.}}{16}{figure.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:audit_transition}{{4}{16}{The energy certificate reduces length while the multiscale normalization tracks loading transitions. The coefficient is $\epsilon =\kappa \tn ^2/\lambda ^2$ in the prespecified loading $A_\epsilon $. Each sample-size cohort contains 2,000 datasets reused across loadings and methods. Bars are pointwise $\pm 1.96$ Monte Carlo standard errors; ratios use paired influence-variable errors. The horizontal line in the right panel is equal mean length. The left panel is normalized by a rate scale, not an exact optimal confidence constant}{figure.4}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {8}Discussion and limitations}{16}{section.8}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Median-localized intervals shrink at higher orders under Gaussian and finite-variance heavy-tailed errors. Each point is a mean over 1,000 datasets with $\pm 1.96$ Monte Carlo standard-error bars. The $t_{2.5}$ errors have variance $0.2^2$ but lack third and fourth moments. Dotted lines show the predicted limiting slopes, while the observed finite-grid slopes remain steeper. The two cap profiles share observations within each noise/sample-size cohort.}}{17}{figure.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:robust_audit}{{5}{17}{Median-localized intervals shrink at higher orders under Gaussian and finite-variance heavy-tailed errors. Each point is a mean over 1,000 datasets with $\pm 1.96$ Monte Carlo standard-error bars. The $t_{2.5}$ errors have variance $0.2^2$ but lack third and fourth moments. Dotted lines show the predicted limiting slopes, while the observed finite-grid slopes remain steeper. The two cap profiles share observations within each noise/sample-size cohort}{figure.5}{}}
